Source: niox
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Nicholas Yoder <nicholasyoder@proton.me>
# cargo/rustc come from rustup (rust-toolchain.toml); Debian's are too old.
# libroc, libpipewire, and libclang (bindgen) are for niox-session's audio.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), pkgconf, libroc-dev (>= 0.4), libpipewire-0.3-dev, libclang-dev
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/nicholasyoder/niox
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: niox
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Share one keyboard and mouse across Linux machines over the LAN
 niox shares one machine's keyboard and mouse with other Linux machines
 over an encrypted UDP transport, switching between them with a hotkey.
 nioxd runs as a system service from boot, so it works at the login screen
 and on a host with nobody logged in. nioxctl shows and controls it.

Package: niox-session
Architecture: any
# The PipeWire server, its session manager, and its PulseAudio replacement,
# so apps' audio reaches the niox sink: libpipewire alone isn't enough.
Depends: niox (= ${binary:Version}), pipewire-audio, ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: niox audio: the optional per-user service
 niox-session is the optional, unprivileged half of niox, run per user
 under systemctl --user. It carries audio over nioxd's encrypted
 transport: a client's system audio plays on its host, mixed with the
 host's other clients or following keyboard/mouse focus. It's a separate
 package so that keyboard/mouse-only installs don't need libroc or
 libpipewire. The user unit is enabled for every user and starts for
 members of the niox group.
